"* oe Jstice THOMAS, With whom Justice Gorsucu joins as to Part II, concurring in the judgment. * Ok * * * The critical question in these cases is what boundaries the Fifth Amendment’s due process guarantee, as originally understood, places on the Federal Government’s power to extend personal jurisdiction over respondents. Historical evidence demonstrates that the answer is “none.” “Because the majority has adopted an analysis that is largely unconnected ) the Constitution’s text and history, I concur only in the judgment.” * * * I * * * The Fifth Amendment’s Due Process Clause provides that “[n]o person” shall “be deprived of life, liberty, or property, without due process of law.” Thus, to show that the PSJVTA’s jurisdictional provisions run afoul of the Fifth Amendment’s due process protections, respondents must establish both that they are “person[s]” protected by the Fifth .mendment and that the PSJVTA transgresses their due process rights. These requirements in turn raise two threshold issues: whether respondents enjoy constitutional rights in the first place, and what “due process of law” requires. While my conclusions as to both threshold issues remain tentative, there are strong reasons to think that each poses an independently ital problem for respondents. * * * A I am skeptical that entities such as the Palestine Liberation Organization (PLO) and the Palestinian Authority (PA) enjoy any constitutional rights at all, let alone qualify as “person[s]” for purposes of the Fifth Amendment. * Ok B Even assuming that the PLO and the PA are “person[s]” protected by the Fifth Amendment, respondents still must grapple with another threshold inquiry: whether the Due Process Clause imposes any limits on the legislative power. a 6. * * * As | have previously explained, the Due Process Clause may have originally been understood to require only that our Government Ct. 2551, 192 L.Ed.2d 569 (2015) (opinion concurring in judgment). * * * Others have disagreed. * * * proceed according to the “law of the land”—that is, according to written constitutional and statutory provisions,’ ” before depriving someone of life, liberty, or property. Johnson v. United States, 576 U.S. 591, 623, 135 * kK If the “law of the land” view of due process is correct and requires only that Congress have authorized the deprivation of life, liberty, or property that the Federal Government effects, * * * then the PSJVTA’s proper enactment resolves the question presented. But, as explained next, even 492 ssuming that “settled usages and modes of proceeding” govern the due process inquiry, * * * respondents’ due process argument still fails. II The Fifth Amendment was never understood to constrain Congress’s ability to extend federal jurisdiction. The Federal Government has always possessed the power to extend its jurisdiction beyond the Nation’s borders, and, as understood in 1791, the Fifth Amendment did not limit this sovereign prerogative. Rather, insofar as any limits on extraterritorial isdiction existed, they stemmed from general principles of international law. But, those principles were defeasible, subconstitutional rules that the sovereign could override through clear command. This understanding respects the Constitution’s design by reserving matters of foreign affairs to the political branches. ok ** * In Picquet v. Swan, 19 F.Cas. 609, 613 (No. 11,134) (CC Mass. 1828), the plaintiff sued a defendant residing abroad through jurisdictionally dubious means, the implications of which might suggest that “a subject of England, or France, or Russia .. . may be summoned from the other end of the globe to obey our process, and submit to the judgment of our ourts.” Justice Story concluded that Congress had not authorized the plaintiff’s expansive theory of federal Jurisdiction, which would interfere with “principles of .. . immutable justice.” * * * But, he made clear that this absence of congressional action was due to a lack of will, not power: “If congress had prescribed such a rule, the court would certainly be bound ) follow it, and proceed upon the law.” * * * This Court eventually endorsed Justice Story’s analysis. In Toland v. Sprague, 12 Pet. 300, 302, 9 L.Ed. 1093 (1838), as in Piequet, the Court addressed whether a federal statute authorized a plaintiff’s attempt to have the federal court exercise personal jurisdiction over a defendant residing abroad. Assigning “great force” to the reasoning of Picquet, the Toland ‘ourt held that Congress had not contemplated federal jurisdiction over those “who were in a foreign Jurisdiction,” and thus had not extended “the reach of the process of the courts” over those individuals. * * * Nevertheless, the Court followed Justice Story’s analysis and concluded that federal courts would be bound to exercise such jurisdiction if Congress >quired it through “positive legislation,” no matter how “unjust.” * * * Subsequent historical and legal developments, including the ratification of the Fourteenth Amendment, have not changed the fundamental point that the Fifth Amendment’s Due Process Clause does not territorially confine the Federal Government’s jurisdiction.3 During the Lochner era, for example, this Court began to determine that the Fourteenth .mendment restricts States* ability to adjudicate cases involving conduct beyond their borders. See, e.g., Baker v. Baker, Eccles & Co., 242 U.S. 394, 403, 37 S.Ct. 152, 61 L.Ed. 386 (1917). But, the Court during that period continued to recognize that the Fifth Amendment provides “no ground for constructing an imaginary constitutional barrier around the exterior onfines of the United States for the purpose of shutting [the federal] government off from the exertion of powers which inherently belong to it by virtue of its sovereignty.” United States v. Bennett, 232 U.S. 299, 306, 34 S.Ct. 433, 58 L.Ed. 612 (1914) * * *. +k That Congress may override general principles of international law does not imply that it shou/d, but instead tha